---
/**
 * The machine.
 *
 * Answers one question: what did formalizing it find?
 *
 * The order of this page is the order of the honesty. Somebody else published
 * a machine-checked proof that Gödel was right. It reproduces. Then: what is
 * that proof actually made of, and what happens if you take Gödel's own idea
 * out of it? That is the ablation, and it is the one result on this site that
 * exists nowhere else.
 *
 * The page ends where the research ends, which is with the reasons this route
 * cannot deliver what it looks like it delivers.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MarginNote from '../components/MarginNote.astro';
import Value from '../components/Value.astro';
import AxiomAblation from '../islands/AxiomAblation.astro';
import { fact } from '../lib/facts';
import { blobUrl } from '../lib/content';

const cost = fact('model.stepone.cost');
const i219 = fact('incident.219');
const vacuous = fact('incident.vacuous_ablation');
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const REPLICATION = 'analysis/united-states-1947/formal-model-replication.md';
const INERT = 'analysis/united-states-1947/inert-manoeuvre.md';
---

<Base
  title="The machine"
  description="Somebody already published a machine-checked proof that Gödel was right. It reproduces. This is what happens when you ask which of its fifty-one axioms the proof is actually using, and what breaks when Gödel's own step one is deleted."
  section="machine"
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="wide lede">
      <p class="kicker smallcaps">Route two</p>
      <h1>Treat the document as a machine, and ask what states it can reach.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">
        Somebody has already done this and published a proof. The interesting part
        is not that it works. It is what the proof turns out to be made of.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="reproduces">
      <h2 id="reproduces">It reproduces</h2>
      <p>
        Zahoransky and Benzmüller built a model of Gödel&#8217;s argument in
        Isabelle/HOL, a proof assistant whose kernel checks every step. It compiles
        clean against Isabelle 2025 and discharges thirty theorems, including the
        three that carry the argument: no dictatorship under the Constitution as it
        stood, none after Article&nbsp;V is amended, and dictatorship reached at the
        third step with no rule broken.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compiling is not reproducing, and the distance between them is the whole
        discipline. An inconsistent axiom set proves every formula, so a theory can
        discharge the dictatorship theorem while carrying no information at all. The
        thesis itself ships a deliberately inconsistent smoke-test theory that proves
        <code>False</code> on purpose, and our harness confirms it still does.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the result was tested three further ways. Nitpick was asked to satisfy the
        axioms, and found a model. It was asked to refute <code>False</code>, and
        found a countermodel, so the axioms do not entail everything. Then it was
        asked for countermodels to the duals of all three targets, and found all
        three. Countermodels are semantic rather than tactical: they show no proof
        can exist, which is stronger than observing that a proof attempt failed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="Replication">
      <p>
        All 110 identifiers in our transcription appear in the thesis. Of 67 labelled
        formulas in the source, 65 appear in ours; the two that do not are an integer
        time representation the thesis introduces only to reject, and a false positive
        on the English word <em>proposition</em>. No axiom was omitted.
      </p>
      <p>
        One deviation is deliberate: the bold-face connectives were renamed to avoid
        colliding with HOL built-ins. That is notation only, and it is the most likely
        site of a silent divergence, so it is flagged rather than buried.
      </p>
      <p><a href={blobUrl(REPLICATION)}>{REPLICATION}</a></p>
    </MarginNote>

    <section aria-labelledby="ablation">
      <h2 id="ablation">Now take it apart</h2>
      <p>
        A proof that checks tells you the conclusion follows. It does not tell you
        what it follows <em>from</em>. The model has <Value id="model.axioms.total" bare
        /> axioms, and the natural question — which ones is the proof using? — is not
        one the paper asks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ablation answers it by deletion. Remove an axiom, ask the machine whether the
        theorem still holds, and ask a model finder whether the reduced theory is still
        consistent. That second question is not optional. An inconsistent theory
        obligingly proves every theorem you were hoping to see.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="wide">
      <AxiomAblation />
    </div>

    <MarginNote
      head="The ablation"
      facts={['model.axioms.total', 'model.axioms.sufficient', 'model.axioms.necessary', 'model.stepone.cost']}
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="what-it-means">
      <h2 id="what-it-means">What that actually means</h2>
      <p>
        Gödel&#8217;s route has two steps. First amend Article&nbsp;V to remove the
        clause protecting equal Senate suffrage. Then use the unentrenched procedure
        to install a dictator. Step one is the idea. It is the part people repeat.
      </p>
      <p>
        Delete the four axioms that carry out step one, and every published theorem
        and every intermediate lemma still proves, in a theory that is still
        consistent. What is lost is exactly one proposition:
        <code>{cost.value}</code> — the ratification event itself — which stops being
        entailed and becomes <em>independent</em>. It does not become false. It becomes
        undetermined, which is what happens when the axioms that forced it are withdrawn.
      </p>
      <p>
        So step one is not free, and it is not load-bearing either. It costs one
        proposition, and no published result uses it.
      </p>
      <p class="correction">
        This is a narrower claim than earlier drafts of this work made. One said the
        model <em>cannot represent</em> repeal at all. Another said deleting step one
        costs <em>nothing</em>. Both were false, adversarial review said so, and the
        machine agreed with the reviewer both times. The corrections are on the record
        in <a href={blobUrl(INERT)}>{INERT.split('/').pop()}</a> rather than quietly
        absorbed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="cannot" class="caveat">
      <h2 id="cannot">Why this route cannot find what we are looking for</h2>
      <p>
        The limitation is structural, not a matter of effort. The model contains
        constitutional text and nothing else.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means it cannot see Austria, which left the constitution entirely and
        governed through an unrepealed 1917 wartime statute, and it cannot see Italy,
        whose Statuto was never amended while the state was dismantled around it.
        <strong>Two of the three collapses on
        <a href={`${base}/precedents/`}>the precedents page</a> are invisible to it in
        principle.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a second gap and it is larger. The <em>effect</em> of amending
        Article&nbsp;V is not derived anywhere in the model; it is implemented by
        omitting an axiom. And the model has no representation of ratification by the
        states at all — the quantity <a href={`${base}/text/`}>the text page</a> is
        entirely about. At <Value id="states.count" bare /> states the requirement is
        <Value id="threshold.states.ratify" bare />. A model that omits the constraint
        cannot report that the constraint is what stops you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read precisely, the formalization proves a conditional: <em>if</em>
        Article&nbsp;V can be amended in the way described, the rest follows by lawful
        steps. The antecedent is the contested part, and it sits outside the model.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="Scope">
      <p>
        This is not a criticism of the paper, which scopes itself to a case example
        and says so. It is a specification for what a successor has to do: the node
        set has to extend past constitutional text to the statutes standing beside it
        and the chamber rules operating inside it.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <section aria-labelledby="tools">
      <h2 id="tools">Two things the tools got right and we got wrong</h2>
      <p>
        A verified result is only as good as the correspondence between the formula and
        the sentence in English attached to it, and that correspondence is the one link
        in the chain no prover checks.
      </p>
      <dl class="incidents">
        <dt>The solver was right and the label was wrong</dt>
        <dd>
          While confirming the thresholds, Z3 printed {i219.value}. The encoding
          minimised <code>yes</code> rather than <code>present</code>, so the larger
          figure was an arbitrary witness among the models achieving the same number of
          yes votes. Nothing was unsound. The formula answered exactly what had been
          written; the English sentence beside it claimed something the formula did not
          say.
        </dd>

        <dt>An ablation that {vacuous.value}</dt>
        <dd>
          Caught by adversarial review rather than by the harness, which is the wrong
          way round. Every ablation on this page now carries a consistency probe, and
          <code>verify.sh</code> fails if one is missing.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <MarginNote head="On the record" facts={['incident.219', 'incident.vacuous_ablation']} />

    <nav class="wide next" aria-label="Continue">
      <p class="smallcaps">Next</p>
      <p>
        None of this is worth anything if you cannot check it.
        <a href={`${base}/method/`}>Here is how to break it</a>.
      </p>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* A belief this project held and abandoned. Set in the disproved colour and
     ruled off, because the retractions are the most persuasive thing here and
     hiding them would be the one unforgivable move. */
  .correction {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--disproved);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .caveat {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    padding: var(--space-md) 0 var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .incidents {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .incidents dt {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
  }

  .incidents dd {
    margin: 0.2em 0 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .next {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-sm);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next .smallcaps {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
  }
</style>
